# CoW view-materialization on the CLOSURE BODY lowering path.
#
# AXIS CELL — path = `emit_closure_call_function` (src/ir/lowering/closures.rs).
# One of eleven function-body-lowering paths, and the only one whose body is a
# bare `Spanned<Expr>` rather than a statement block — `FnBodyAst::Expr` is what
# carries it into the prescans. It used to lower with an EMPTY
# `cow_reassigned_after`, so the element view stayed a lazy borrow and the 64
# pushes reallocated it away.
#
# ⚠ THE VECTOR IS LOCAL TO THE CLOSURE, DELIBERATELY. This cell is about the
# closure BODY reaching the per-function prescans. The sibling shape where the
# vector is CAPTURED was a DIFFERENT defect with a different mechanism and a
# different fix — the capture site owed the consuming-position rule (clone a
# live source, move a dead one) and did neither, so the environment aliased a
# buffer the source still owned. That was `t0704`; it is CLOSED, and its cells
# are live fixtures next to this one (`closure_capture_then_mutate_source_uaf`,
# `closure_capture_inside_body_uaf`).
# The discriminator that made the two separate is still the point of this file:
# when the prescan fix landed, no-capture went rc 139 -> rc 0 and BOTH capture
# shapes stayed rc 139. Do not "generalise" this fixture by hoisting the vector
# out of the closure — it would stop testing this cell and start testing the
# capture boundary, which the two fixtures above already cover.
#
# THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING (64 pushes), and the payload is heap-FORCED via
# `mk` so ASan reports a legible heap-use-after-free, not a bare SIGSEGV.
#
# RED-VERIFIED against the pre-fix compiler: rc 139 SIGSEGV on C AND LLVM.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

void main():
    auto f = ():
        Vector[String] v = [mk("hello", "world")]
        String s = v.get(0).unwrap()
        int i = 0
        while i < 64:
            v.push(mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy"))
            i = i + 1
        print(s)
    f()
